Year = {1997},
Pages = {71-82},
Note = {3rd International Interdisciplinary Conference on the Environment,
   WORCESTER, MA, JUN 25-28, 1997},
Organization = {Interdisciplinary Environm Assoc},
Abstract = {Energy issues are at the heart of many of our contemporary environmental
   problems. The use of fossil fuels has been blamed for global climate
   change and acid rain. Cheap fossil fuels have fostered the expanded use
   of automobiles and facilitated the mass urban sprawl which has destroyed
   and degraded wetlands and led to a loss of agricultural land. Increased
   reliance on fossil fuels has led to dramatic changes in the nature of
   agriculture and threatens our current and future systems of food
   production. Thus, the development of alternative energy systems would
   appear to be the key to the development of sustainable economic systems.
   This paper looks at the potential for the development of alternative
   energy systems in the Northeastern Ohio Region and examines the barriers
   to the spread of these technologies. By focusing on recent debates
   surrounding proposals for the construction of a new power line, it
   illustrates that renewable energy systems offer better solutions to
   traditional problems of power reliability and potential growth in the
   region. It concludes that the barriers to the development of such
   sustainable energy systems are primarily social and political rather
   than scientific or economic and discusses proposals for the reduction of
   structural barriers in the electric utility industry.},
