   the 21st Century, RHODES, GREECE, SEP 08-10, 1999},
Organization = {Wessex Inst Technol},
Abstract = {Economic performances of Italian local systems of small and medium
   enterprise have been considered of the utmost interest by foreign and
   Italian scholars.
   This paper aims at analysing the relation between the Italian pattern of
   ``industrial district{''} and transport/communication services, as well
   as accessibility levels. Our purpose is also to investigate the
   influence of accessibility levels on the economic performance of
   districts, and to survey whether an high accessibility is a key location
   factor affecting the rise of a district, or, on the contrary, the
   factors giving rise to a district are effective despite a poor level of
   accessibility and of transport/communication facilities. The analysis
   has been carried out by comparing Italian districts with metropolitan
   areas, and administrative Regions and Provinces, from the points of view
   of geographical position and Location Quotients (LQ). The analysis shows
   that examined industrial districts have no remarkable comparative
   advantage so far as accessibility is concerned and have a tendency to
   search and buy outside their needs in transport services for goods and
   passengers, as well as in communication services.},
