   25-27, 2008},
Organization = {Int Soc Mind, Brain \& Educ; SE Univ, Key Lab Child Dev \& Learning Sci;
   Thinktank - Handsbrain Educ; SE Univ State Key Lab Bioelect; Chinese Soc
   Biomed Elect},
Abstract = {. The study explored the relation between paternal beliefs and parenting
   practices related to friendship. Fathers reported their perceptions of
   function of friendship for their own and parenting practices of child
   friendships. The results suggested that paternal beliefs can impact his
   parenting practices through two ways: one is directly from paternal
   perceptions of his own friendship (emotional support) to parenting
   practices (guidance and rules) and another one is from paternal
   perceptions of his own friendship to his. attitude to child's friendship
   and then to parenting practices.' We also found two distinct effects of
   perception-attitude interaction which suggested that father's attitude
   toward child's friendship could play as a moderator between fathers'
   perceived function of friendship and their prohibiting behaviors of
   child's friendship.},
