Note = {5th International Conference on Urban Transport and the Environment for
   the 21st Century, RHODES, GREECE, SEP 08-10, 1999},
Organization = {Wessex Inst Technol},
Abstract = {A funded research project was conducted during the period July 1992
   through November 1994. The project was designed to evaluate indoor and
   ambient air quality in and around buildings of different types and uses
   in Riyadh, the capital of Saudi Arabia. Thirty intercity buildings and
   two outercity (background) sites were carefully selected and monitored
   for air quality. Ten air pollutants, together with relevant
   meteorological parameters, were monitored indoor and outdoor at each
   site continuously and simultaneously for a period of two weeks covering
   summer and winter seasons.
   This paper presents the results obtained for three pollutants (TSP, Pb,
   and CO). The investigation revealed TSP and Pb levels that were
   unacceptable and exceeded the standards indoor and outdoor in most
   monitored locations. CO levels, on the other hand, showed acceptable
   levels at most locations, with the exception of few sites located at
   extremely traffic congested locations. Outdoor pollution sources were
   the major contributors to indoor pollution, violations of the standards
   were pronounced in summer than in winter. Statistical and frequency
   analyses were performed on the collected data, showing seasonal and
   sector by sector variability, and outdoor-indoor correlations.},
