   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci Intro;
   Natl Acad Sci Ukraine; Natl Acad Sci Armenia; Sci Counc Japan; World
   Acad Sci TWAS; European Acad Sci, Arts \& Lett; Acad Fine Arts Zagreb
   Croatia; Croatian Acad Sci \& Arts; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad
   Sci \& Arts; Georgian Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Russian Acad Arts; Turkish Acad Sci; SGEM},
Abstract = {This study aimed to investigate the way Psychology students (N = 161)
   are constructing and modifying a mental model regarding the
   Neuropsychology course. Following previous studies (Albrecht and
   O'Brien, 1993; Radvansky, Spieler \& Zacks, 1993; Slama-Cazacu, 2002),
   we elaborated a questionnaire comprising a set of mental dimensions
   necessary for information processing (memory, emotion processing,
   motivation, temperament, character, context processing). For assessing
   their mental model, subjects were asked to answer, on a 5-step Likert
   scale, about the way they mentally represent, information, emotions,
   schemas and context (internal and external) for the Neuropsychology
   course. The questionnaire was administered two times: before the classes
   started and after the final exam. Results from the first administration
   were collected and analyzed in order to adjust the information and
   teaching to the collective mental model. The changes, if any, were
   measured via a second administration of the same instrument.
   The first administration of the instrument had high values for explicit
   context (m = 4.1/5), temperament (m = 4.2/5) and motivation (m = 4.2/5)
   and low values (less then 2.5/5) for semantic memory and teaching style.
   A conceptual map was taken into account, for the correction of the
   initial scores via taught information and teaching style. The second
   administration of the questionnaire showed significantly improved values
   for semantic memory, internal context and motivation (p < .05 for each),
   compared to the first administration, and lowered levels of temperament
   and emotion (not statistically significant). Results support the idea of
   changeability of the mental model in educational process through
   psychological analysis of the components involved in its structure. This
   instrument could be a useful tool for teachers interested in the quality
   of the act of teaching, the identification of powerful ways of forming
   solid conceptual base for each discipline},
